1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,900
Good morning. Today I'd like to present the findings of our Year 2 project on wildlife found in gardens throughout our city.

2
00:00:06,900 --> 00:00:10,518
I'll start by saying something about the background to the project,

3
00:00:10,518 --> 00:00:13,420
then talk a little bit about our research techniques

4
00:00:13,520 --> 00:00:16,110
and then indicate some of our interim findings.

5
00:00:16,110 --> 00:00:19,200
First of all, how did we choose our topic?

6
00:00:19,200 --> 00:00:21,740
Well, there are four of us in the group

7
00:00:21,800 --> 00:00:25,119
and one day while we were discussing a possible focus,

8
00:00:25,119 --> 00:00:27,923
two of the group mentioned that they had seen

9
00:00:27,923 --> 00:00:31,560
yet more sparrow-hawks -- one of Britain's most interesting birds of prey

10
00:00:31,600 --> 00:00:33,220
in their own city centre gardens

11
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:37,270
and wondered why they were turning up in these gardens in great numbers.

12
00:00:37,270 --> 00:00:43,400
We were all very engaged by the idea of why wild animals would choose to inhabit a city garden.

13
00:00:43,400 --> 00:00:49,190
Why is it so popular with wildlife when the countryside itself is becoming less so?

14
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:56,250
The first thing we did was to establish what proportion of the urban land is taken up by private gardens.

15
00:00:56,250 --> 00:00:59,420
We estimated that it was about one fifth,

16
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:06,950
and this was endorsed by looking at large-scale usage maps in the town land survey office-24% to be precise.

17
00:01:06,950 --> 00:01:09,322
Our own informal discussions with neighbours

18
00:01:09,322 --> 00:01:16,832
and friends led us to believe that many garden owners had interesting experiences to relate regarding wild animal sightings,

19
00:01:16,832 --> 00:01:21,970
so we decided to survey garden owners from different areas of the city.

20
00:01:21,970 --> 00:01:31,820
Just over 100 of them completed a survey once every two weeks for twelve months-ticking off species they had seen from a pro forma list

21
00:01:32,100 --> 00:01:34,460
and adding the names of any rarer ones.

22
00:01:34,460 --> 00:01:39,200
Meanwhile, we were doing our own observations in selected gardens throughout the city.

23
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:41,820
We deliberately chose smaller ones

24
00:01:41,820 --> 00:01:44,100
because they were by far the most typical in the city.

25
00:01:44,100 --> 00:01:48,430
The whole point of the project was to look at the norm not the exception.

26
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:50,997
Alongside this primary research on urban gardens,

27
00:01:50,997 --> 00:01:56,863
we were studying a lot of books about the decline of wild animals in the countryside

28
00:01:56,863 --> 00:01:59,430
and thinking of possible causes for this.

29
00:01:59,430 --> 00:02:01,140
So what did we find

30
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:05,120
Well, so much that I just won't have time to tell you about here.

31
00:02:05,120 --> 00:02:08,151
If you're interested in reading our more comprehensive findings,

32
00:02:08,151 --> 00:02:11,182
we've produced detailed graphic representations on the college web-site

33
00:02:11,182 --> 00:02:16,570
and of course any of the group would be happy to talk to you about them.

34
00:02:16,570 --> 00:02:28,170
Just email us. What we've decided to present today is information about just three species-because we felt these gave a good indication of the processes at work in rural

35
00:02:28,170 --> 00:02:30,570
and urban settings as a whole.

36
00:02:30,570 --> 00:02:38,150
The first species to generate a lot of interesting information was frogs.

37
00:02:38,150 --> 00:02:40,360
And there was a clear pattern here

38
00:02:40,580 --> 00:02:43,040
they proliferate where there is suitable water.

39
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:47,400
Garden ponds are on the increase, rural ponds are disappearing

40
00:02:47,860 --> 00:02:50,000
leading to massive migration to the towns.

41
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:54,515
Hedgehogs are also finding it easier to live in urban areas -- this time

42
00:02:54,515 --> 00:02:57,380
because their predators are not finding it quite

43
00:02:57,380 --> 00:02:59,724
so attractive to leave their rural environment,

44
00:02:59,724 --> 00:03:02,850
so hedgehogs have a better survival rate in cities.

45
00:03:02,850 --> 00:03:04,469
We had lots of sightings,

46
00:03:04,469 --> 00:03:09,650
so all in all we had no difficulties with our efforts to count their numbers precisely.

47
00:03:09,650 --> 00:03:15,240
Our final species is the finest of bird singers, the song thrush.

48
00:03:15,240 --> 00:03:17,360
On the decline in the countryside

49
00:03:18,200 --> 00:03:20,251
they are experiencing a resurgence in urban gardens

50
00:03:20,251 --> 00:03:23,831
because these days gardeners are buying lots of different plants

51
00:03:23,831 --> 00:03:29,200
which means there's an extensive range of seeds around, which is what they feed on.

52
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:35,259
Another factor is the provision of nesting places-which is actually better in gardens than the countryside.

53
00:03:35,259 --> 00:03:37,910
Hard to believe it, but it's true.

54
00:03:37,910 --> 00:03:42,832
Incidentally, we discovered that a massive new survey on song thrushes is about to be launched,

55
00:03:42,832 --> 00:03:45,600
so you should keep an eye open for that.

56
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:48,530
Now, I'd be happy to answer any questions you may have...
